---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const gammaCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: '1/sqrt(1 - x*x)', label: 'Lorentz factor γ', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.4 }],
  x: { min: 0, max: 0.99, label: 'speed as fraction of light speed, v/c' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 8, label: 'γ (time & length factor)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/advanced/special-relativity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Einstein's 1905 theory starts from one stubborn fact: <strong>light travels at the same speed
    for every observer</strong>, no matter how they move. Keep that and drop "time is the same for
    everyone" — and the consequences cascade. Moving clocks run slow, moving lengths shrink, and
    nothing with mass reaches light speed, because reaching it takes infinite energy.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Lorentz factor — gentle at highway speeds, brutal above 90% of light speed" widget="plotter" config={gammaCfg} />

  <p>
    γ (gamma) is the multiplier: a clock moving at 87% of light speed ticks at 1/2 rate (γ = 2);
    at 99.5%, γ = 10. The curve is lazy for almost all of daily life — GPS satellites need only
    the tiniest corrections — then rockets upward as v → c. That wall <em>is</em> the cosmic
    speed limit.
  </p>

  <h2>The consequences, ranked by strangeness</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Time dilation is real and measured:</strong> muons created in the upper atmosphere reach the ground because their internal clocks run slow (by γ ≈ 29); atomic clocks flown around the world disagree with stay-home clocks exactly as predicted (Hafele–Keating, 1971).</li>
    <li><strong>Relativity of simultaneity:</strong> "at the same time" is observer-dependent — two events simultaneous for one observer are sequential for a moving one. There is no universal now.</li>
    <li><strong>E = mc²:</strong> mass is a form of energy. A gram of matter is 9 × 10¹³ joules — the physics of stars, reactors and why particle accelerators must push energy, not just speed, to go further.</li>
    <li><strong>Nothing travels faster than c:</strong> not because engines are weak, but because γ → ∞: infinite energy, zero proper time. c is not primarily about light — it is the conversion rate between space and time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Special relativity handles constant speeds; acceleration and gravity demanded ten more years
    and geometry — the general theory, later in this ladder.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Einstein, "Zur Elektrodynamik bewegter Körper" (1905); Hafele & Keating, Science (1972).</p>
</TopicPage>
